# SLUG.ai v0.3
# Purpose: AI-targeted, informationally dense language + runtime contract.
# Style: line-oriented statements; stable keys; low prose; no tutorials.

@section identity
language slug
slug-version unknown
spec-version 0.3
timezone default
notes "This file is authoritative for stable language semantics, builtins, core runtime behavior, baseline stdlib expectations, and MANIFEST.ai requirements."

@section philosophy
law immutability "All runtime values are immutable; any 'change' produces a new value."
law explicit-imports "Everything is a module; values are imported explicitly; there are no implicit globals."
law lexical-scope "Lexical scope with block-level bindings; shadowing allowed."
law recursion-over-loops "No loop statements; iteration is expressed via recursion + match + reducers."
law deep-modules "Prefer small stable public APIs that hide complexity behind module contracts."
law contract-first "Public contracts live in MANIFEST.ai; source is not the contract."
law deterministic-diffs "Generated artifacts are stable-sorted and whitespace-stable for clean diffs."
law ai-readable-contracts "Language and library contracts should be explicit enough that agents do not need to infer missing behavior."

@section comments
comment-prefix "#" "for shell scripts"
comment-prefix "//" "std comment"
comment-block "/* ..."
comment-block "
/**
 * doc comment
 */
"

@section execution-model
eval model expression-oriented
eval statements "program is a sequence of statements separated by NEWLINE or ';' (ASI-style)."
eval top-level "top-level statements execute on module load (including imports)."
eval module-load "import executes top-level of imported module."
eval module-cache "a module's top-level statements execute at most once per process; subsequent imports reuse the cached module."
eval running-module "the running module (root module passed to the runner) executes: (1) its top-level statements, then (2) its @main function if present."
eval main-on-import "@main never executes on import; it only executes when the module is the running module."
eval main-error "if top-level execution raises an error, @main is not executed."
eval return "return exits the nearest function body immediately."
eval throw "throw raises an error value (value shape is user-defined; commonly struct(Error))."
eval defer "defer registers cleanup logic; defer actions unwind at scope exit on normal return and error paths."
eval block-value "a block evaluates to the value of its final expression, or nil if no value-producing final expression exists."
eval fn-return "a function returns the value produced by its body unless exited earlier by explicit return."

@section values
type primitive nil bool num str bytes sym fn task struct map list
type summary "Primary runtime value kinds are nil, bool, num, str, bytes, sym, fn, task, struct, map, list."
sym literal ":ident or :\"any string\""
bytes literal "0x\"<hex>\""
number literal "decimal or hex; underscores allowed; decimal supports fractional and exponent forms."
str standard "double-quoted strings \"...\" support {{expr}} interpolation and backslash escape sequences."
str interpolation "{{expr}} inside double-quoted or triple-double-quoted strings; expr is a full expression evaluated and coerced to string."
str raw "single-quoted strings '...' are raw; no interpolation; no escape processing; backslashes are literal."
str multiline "triple-double-quoted strings \"\"\"...\"\"\" are multiline; support {{expr}} interpolation; preserve interior newlines."
str raw-multiline "triple-single-quoted strings '''...''' are raw multiline; no interpolation; no escape processing."
str raw-use "prefer raw strings for SQL, regex, and content containing backslashes or {{ braces."
value equality "== and != compare values by value semantics."
value truthiness "Only false and nil are falsey unless otherwise specified by runtime."
map-keys "In map literals, bare identifiers as keys are coerced to symbols (k:1 == :k:1)."
string interpolation "{{expr}} inside any string literal or multi-line string evaluates expr and inserts its string representation; e.g. \"Hello {{name}}!\" or \"error: {{err.msg}}\""
string multiline "'''...''' is a multi-line string literal; all content between the delimiters is included verbatim including newlines."
string multiply "@str * @num repeats a string n times: \" \" * 3 == \"   \""

@section indexing-and-slicing
index supported "indexing and slicing are supported for list, str, bytes, and map where applicable."
index base "indices are zero-based."
index list "list[i] returns the element at i."
index str "str[i] returns a single-character string."
index bytes "bytes[i] returns a numeric byte value."
index map "map[k] performs exact key lookup using k as-is; no implicit coercion at lookup time."
index negative "negative indices are not supported as standalone index expressions."
index oob "out-of-bounds indexing is a runtime error."
slice form "x[a:b] and x[a:b:c] are valid for sliceable values."
slice half-open "slice end is exclusive."
slice omit-start "x[:b] is valid."
slice omit-end "x[a:] is valid."
slice negative-index "negative values in slice positions are valid: x[-4:] returns the last 4 elements; x[0:-1] omits the last element."
slice step "x[a:b:c] uses start:end:step."
slice negative-step "negative step is unsupported unless explicitly documented by runtime."
slice type "slicing returns the same collection/string/bytes family as the source value."

@section bindings
binding var "var <pattern> = <expr> binds name(s); allows reassignment via identifier assignment."
binding val "val <pattern> = <expr> binds name(s); disallows reassignment."
binding var-preference "strongly prefer val over var; use var only when a binding genuinely requires reassignment within its scope; val is the default."
assignment "identifier = expr is allowed; assignment targets identifiers only."
assignment disallowed "field assignment, index assignment, and in-place mutation are not part of Slug."
pattern "_" "wildcard pattern; binds nothing."
pattern binding "name@pattern binds name to matched value while matching pattern."
pattern spread "...name or ... in list/map patterns; list spread must be final."
pattern pinned "^name matches against the existing binding of name."
pattern list "[p1, p2, ...] supports spread; common recursive shape is [h, ...t]."
pattern map "{ k:pat, ... } supports spread."
pattern map-exact "{| k:pat, ... |} is exact map matching; no extra keys."
pattern struct "TypeName{field, field:pat} matches struct values."
pattern struct-destructure "val TypeName{ field, field: alias } = expr destructures a struct value into named bindings in a val or var declaration."
pattern multi "match cases may contain alternative patterns; alternative patterns must be non-binding."
pattern failure "failed pattern match does not bind partial names."
pattern import-wildcard "val {*} = import(...) binds all exported names from the imported modules into the current scope; val { f, g } = import(...) binds only the named exports."

@section functions
fn literal "fn(params) { block } or fn(params) match { cases }"
fn params "parameter := {tag} ['...'] ident ['=' default-expr]; variadic '...' must be last."
fn spread-call "...list in a call expression spreads a list value into variadic arguments: f(...xs) passes each element of xs as a separate argument; equivalent to f(xs[0], xs[1], ...); use when a @list value needs to be passed to a ...rest variadic parameter."
fn defaults "default parameter expressions are evaluated in normal lexical scope."
fn body "function bodies are expression-oriented blocks or match bodies."
fn recur "recur(args...) is the canonical self tail-call form."
fn tco "Tail-call optimization is expected for recur and may apply to eligible tail calls (implementation-defined)."
fn arity "function dispatch accounts for arity and type hints."
fn arity "functions have fixed arity unless variadic."
fn values "functions are first-class values."
fn inline-chain "fn(params) { block } is a valid call-chain value; value /> fn(x) { transform(x) } applies the anonymous function as a chain step; use for short single-expression transforms only."
fn nursery-decorator "nursery [limit N] fn(params) { block } creates a function whose body runs inside a nursery scoped to that call; limit N caps concurrent child tasks spawned within."
fn recur-arity "recur must pass all parameters of the enclosing function including those with defaults; omitting any parameter is a runtime error."

@section call-chain-threading
op call-chain "/> is the call-chain operator: a /> f(x) means f(a, x)."
op chaining "a /> f() /> g(x) threads the previous value as first argument."
op chain-parens "parentheses are optional for single-parameter calls in a chain: x /> trim /> toLower is equivalent to x /> trim() /> toLower()."
op chain-style "prefer /> call chains over nested function calls for sequential data transforms; write x /> f /> g not g(f(x))."
op chain-multiline "prefer one chain step per line when the chain is longer than two steps."
op chain-avoid "avoid chaining across complex branching or nested match expressions; break into named val bindings instead; exception: /> match { } as a terminal decision boundary is idiomatic."
op dot "postfix .ident selects a field/member."
op dot-map "m.foo uses dot lookup tolerance: tries symbol key :foo first, then string key \"foo\"; see maps-and-symbols section."
op dot-struct "s.field selects a struct field."
op call "postfix (args) calls a callable."
op index "postfix [expr] or [a:b[:c]] indexes or slices."

@section operators
precedence lowest "assignment (=)"
precedence "||"
precedence "&&"
precedence "== !="
precedence "< <= > >="
precedence "| ^ &"
precedence "<< >>"
precedence "+ -"
precedence "* / %"
precedence ":+ +:"  # list concat operators
precedence prefix "! - ~"
precedence highest "postfix: call (), index [], dot ., struct-init {}, struct-copy copy{}"
list-op ":+" "append right (list :+ item)"
list-op "+:" "prepend left (item +: list)"
note "There is no mutating update operator; use persistent update helpers."

@section conditionals-and-match
if form "if (expr) {block} else {block|if}"
if value "if is an expression."
match expr "match [expr] { pattern [if guard] => body ... }"
match subject "match requires an explicit scrutinee expression except when used as a pipeline stage (value /> match { ... }); match { ... } without a subject is only valid in pipeline position."
match pipeline "match without an explicit scrutinee is only valid as a pipeline stage: value /> match { ... }; the prior pipeline value is consumed as the scrutinee; it is not valid as a standalone statement or expression."
match pipeline-use "pipelines are for value transformation; /> match { } marks the point where a single value fans out into distinct outcomes; use match as a terminal decision boundary where meaning diverges."
match case-body "case body may be a single expression or block."
match guard "case may include 'if <expr>' guard."
match returns "match is an expression; exactly one case produces the result."
match exhaustiveness "non-exhaustive match is a runtime error unless a fallback case handles remaining inputs."
match top-level "top-level match expressions are valid statements; val _ = match ... is not required."
match arm-map "a map literal returned directly from a match arm body must be wrapped in extra braces to avoid ambiguity with block syntax: => {{ k:1 }} not => { k:1 }"
match arm-braces "multi-statement match arm bodies always require braces: pattern => { stmt; stmt } not pattern => stmt; stmt."

@section statements-and-separators
stmt separator "NEWLINE and ';' terminate statements."
stmt semicolon "semicolon is optional where NEWLINE already terminates the statement."
stmt newline-sensitivity "newline may terminate statements unless parser expects continuation."
stmt style "prefer one logical statement per line in authored code; generators may use ';' where needed for compactness or parser clarity."
stmt intermediate "intermediate expressions in a block are valid statements and do not need to be bound to _; only the final expression of a block produces the block's value."
stmt return-use "return is valid inside a function body for early exit; prefer expression-oriented match/if over return where possible, but use return when it clearly reduces nesting."

@section maps-and-symbols
map literal "{ k:1, :name:2, \"x\":3 } is valid; bare keys become symbols."
map literal "a map literal returned directly from a match arm body must be wrapped in an extra set of braces to avoid ambiguity with the block syntax: '=> { { k:1, :name:2 } }' not '=> { k:1, :name:2 }'."
map symbol-key "k:1 is equivalent to :k:1 in a map literal."
map string-key "\"k\":1 uses a string key; it is distinct from symbol :k."
map dynamic-key "{ [expr]: value } is used for computed keys if supported by grammar/runtime."
map lookup "m[k] uses exact k value; no coercion or fallback at lookup time."
map dot-access "m.foo uses dot lookup tolerance: attempts symbol key :foo first; if absent, attempts string key \"foo\"; if both absent, returns nil; if both present, symbol takes precedence."
map dot-access-strict "dot lookup tolerance applies to dot access only; bracket lookups m[:foo] and m[\"foo\"] remain exact with no coercion or fallback."
map dot-json "prefer dot access (m.name) when working with JSON-decoded maps; slug.json#decode produces string-keyed maps so m[:name] will always return nil on decoded data."
map trailing-comma "trailing commas in map literals are permitted."
map absence "missing map key returns nil unless a stricter runtime mode is documented elsewhere."
sym purpose "symbols are stable lightweight identity keys commonly used for internal records, tags, and metadata."

@section structs
struct schema "struct { [@tag] field ['=' default], ... } defines a struct schema value."
struct init "Schema{field:expr,...} constructs a struct value."
struct field-defaults "omitted fields use schema defaults where defined."
struct copy "value copy{field:expr,...} returns a new value with updated fields."
struct copy-bulk "value copy data where data is a map performs a bulk field update from the map's keys; only keys matching field names are applied."
struct field-access "s.field reads a field."
struct immutability "struct fields cannot be updated in place."
struct role "structs provide schema-aware immutable records."
struct matching "struct patterns match by struct type and field patterns."

@section persistent-updates
update map-put "put(map, key, value) -> map"
update map-remove "remove(map, key) -> map"
update map-update "update(map, key, fn) -> map"
update struct-copy "value copy{field:expr,...} -> same struct type"
update struct-copy-bulk "value copy data -> copies all fields from map data into struct; schema{} copy data constructs a new instance from a schema."
update list-style "list growth is typically expressed via :+ and +: or via library helpers returning new lists."
update guidance "prefer persistent update helpers over reconstructing larger values manually when helpers exist."

@section errors
error canonical "slug.std commonly exports struct(Error) as the canonical error payload."
error throw "throw raises a value as an error."
error runtime "runtime failures such as bad index or invalid operation raise runtime errors."
error catch "try/catch is not part of the language surface."
error contract "public APIs should prefer one documented error style consistently."

@section concurrency
concurrency model "structured concurrency"
nursery form "nursery [limit expr] (fn(...) {block} | {block})"
nursery meaning "a nursery scopes child tasks; child tasks are awaited or cancelled on nursery exit (implementation-defined details)."
spawn form "spawn {block} | spawn <expr>"
spawn result "spawn returns a task handle."
await form "await taskExpr retrieves task result (or error)."
task type "task values represent running computations."
channels "channels are provided by stdlib/runtime modules; use documented channel APIs only."
select form "select { case ... }"
select case "recv chanExpr | send chanExpr, valueExpr | after durationExpr | await taskExpr | _"
select action "a case may transform or bind its result before producing a branch value."
timeouts "after is the standard timeout/select deadline arm."
concurrency rule "avoid unscoped background work; use nursery/spawn/await/select."
defer basic "defer { block } runs on scope exit regardless of normal return or error."
defer onsuccess "defer onsuccess { block } runs on normal return only; does not run if an error is thrown."
defer onerror "defer onerror(err) { block } runs on the error path only; the block's final expression becomes the function's return value, replacing the thrown error; can rethrow with throw or return a replacement value."
defer onerror-pattern "defer onerror is the idiomatic error-handling boundary: use it to clean up resources on failure, record failures, or convert errors into return values."

@section builtins-core
# Builtins listed here are authoritative.
# Agents MUST NOT assume unstated builtins exist.

builtin import(...modules) -> @map
builtin len(v) -> @num
builtin print(...args) -> ?
builtin println(...args) -> ?
builtin stacktrace(error) -> @str
builtin argv() -> @list
builtin argm() -> @map
builtin cfg(@str key, default) -> ?

@section builtins-cli-contract
builtin argv desc "argv() returns positional CLI arguments only; it does not include the executable or script name unless explicitly documented otherwise by runtime."
builtin argm desc "argm() returns parsed CLI options as a map."
builtin argm shape "argm() key/value shape is runtime-defined; agents should only rely on shape when documented by stdlib/runtime."
builtin cfg desc "cfg(key, default) returns configuration value for key, or default when absent."

@section standard-library-baseline
stdlib baseline "The following modules/functions are commonly expected and should be preferred when present in MANIFEST.ai."

stdlib module "slug.std"
stdlib module-role "general-purpose core helpers, formatting, common runtime contracts, canonical Error shape"

stdlib module "slug.list"
stdlib module-role "list transforms and reducers"

stdlib module "slug.string"
stdlib module-role "string transforms, splitting, joining, char-list helpers"

stdlib module "slug.map"
stdlib module-role "persistent map update helpers"

stdlib module "slug.io.fs"
stdlib module-role "filesystem I/O"

stdlib expectation "Agents must confirm exact function names/signatures via MANIFEST.ai when available."

@section stdlib-common-contracts
# These are baseline expectations, not permission to invent undocumented functions in a real library.
common fn "slug.map#put(map, key, value) -> map"
common fn "slug.map#remove(map, key) -> map"
common fn "slug.map#update(map, key, fn) -> map"
common fn "slug.list#reduce(list, init, fn) -> any"
common fn "slug.list#map(list, fn) -> list"
common fn "slug.list#filter(list, fn) -> list"
common fn "slug.string#join(list, sep, empty?) -> str"
common fn "slug.string#asList(str, start?, acc?) -> list"
common fn "slug.io.fs#readFile(path) -> (str|Error)"
common fn "slug.io.fs#writeFile(path, content) -> (?|Error)"

@section fmt-spec
# ADR-024: slug.std#fmt — authoritative formatting contract
fmt signature "fmt(@str format, ...args) -> @str"
fmt placement "lives in slug.std; returns string; performs no IO"
fmt placeholder-auto "{} consumes the next unused positional argument (auto-incrementing cursor)."
fmt placeholder-indexed "{N} reads argument at 0-based index N without advancing the {} cursor."
fmt placeholder-mixed "{} and {N} may be mixed freely in the same format string."
fmt spec-fixed "{:.Nf} renders exactly N fractional digits using DEC64 rounding."
fmt spec-integer "{:d} is integer presentation; equivalent to {:.0f}; uses DEC64 half-even rounding."
fmt spec-percent "{:%} multiplies value by 100 and appends %; precision controls decimal digits."
fmt spec-grouping "{:,} enables thousands separator on integer portion; combinable: {:,.2f}."
fmt spec-align "alignment: < left, > right, ^ center; width is a positive integer: {:>10} {:.<10} {:^10}."
fmt spec-padding "padding uses spaces; no truncation if value exceeds width."
fmt escape "\\{ renders { and \\} renders }; avoids collision with {{expr}} string interpolation."
fmt error "unescaped { not starting a valid placeholder is a runtime error."
fmt error "missing positional arg, out-of-range index, invalid spec, unsupported verb are runtime errors."
fmt vs-interpolation "prefer {{expr}} interpolation for simple substitution; use fmt() when format specs are needed."

@section tags-and-annotations
tag form "@ident or @ident(expr,...)"
tag decl-prefix "tags may prefix var, val, and foreign declarations."
tag params "tags may appear in parameter lists."
tag export "@export marks a symbol as public; exported symbols appear in MANIFEST.ai."
tag main "@main marks a function as the module entrypoint; executes only when the module is the running module, never on import; the function must be callable with zero arguments (default parameters satisfy this); a module may have at most one @main; compilation fails if multiple @main are defined or if the annotated binding is not a zero-callable function."
tag main-pattern "idiomatic @main uses default parameters to receive runtime inputs, keeping the function testable: @main val main = fn(args = argm()) { ... }"
tag returns "@returns('...') documents return type in a stable, parseable form."
tag test "@test and @testWith(...) are runtime-visible metadata for test runners and tooling."
tag testWith "@testWith([inputs...], expected, [inputs...], expected, ...) pairs are interleaved; each [inputs] list contains positional arguments; expected is the return value; multiple pairs test multiple cases on one function."
tag effects "@effects('fs io ...') documents side-effect labels for a function; space-separated; rendered in MANIFEST.ai."
tag throws "@throws('@type') documents a thrown error type; rendered under errors: block in MANIFEST.ai."
tag throwsDesc "@throwsDesc('...') pairs with @throws to provide a cause description for the thrown error."
tag category "tags divide into parser/runtime directives and runtime metadata."
tag docs "documentation-related tags or metadata may be surfaced by slug.meta.describe or related tooling."

@section grammar-summary
grammar source "slug.ebnf is the full grammar source of truth; this summary is normative for agents."
grammar program "program := {stmtSep} {statement {stmtSep}} EOF"
grammar stmtSep "stmtSep := ';' | NEWLINE"
grammar statement "decl | foreign_decl | return | throw | defer | expr"
grammar foreign "foreign name = fn(params)"
grammar decl "tags? (var pattern = expr | val pattern = expr | foreign name = fn(params))"
grammar expr "assignment | logical | comparison | arithmetic | call-chain | postfix | primary"
grammar primary "literal | ident | (expr) | if | match | select | fn | list | map | struct | recur | nursery | spawn"

@section manifest-ai
manifest file "library root MUST contain MANIFEST.ai defining the complete public contract."
manifest load "to satisfy import('slug.x'), tools load <libraryRoot>/MANIFEST.ai and filter fqname prefix 'slug.x'."
manifest format "MANIFEST.ai is line-oriented; comments start with '#'; optional @section headers allowed; fn entries may be followed by indented sub-lines."
manifest required "MANIFEST.ai MUST enumerate all @export symbols; MUST be stable-sorted; SHOULD be generator-owned."
manifest kinds "module | fn | const | struct | sum | type | desc | effects | errors"
manifest fn "fn fqname(params) -> type"
manifest fn-block "fn entries may be followed by indented sub-lines: desc: \"...\", effects: label, errors: with thrown:/returned: entries."
manifest desc "desc: \"micro contract\" as an indented sub-line under a fn or struct entry; first paragraph of the doc comment."
manifest effects "effects: pure|io|db|net|fs|time|random as an indented sub-line under a fn entry."
manifest errors-block "errors: block contains indented thrown: @type [\"cause\"] and returned: @type lines."
manifest struct-fields "struct field lines field <n> [@type][: \"desc\"] are only emitted when a @type or @desc tag is present on the field."
manifest unknown "unknown statement keywords are ignored by parsers for forward compatibility."

@section non-goals
not-supported loops "for, while, and do-style loops are not part of Slug."
not-supported mutation "in-place mutation of map/list/struct contents is not part of Slug."
not-supported field-assign "x.y = z is not supported."
not-supported index-assign "x[i] = z is not supported."
not-supported classes "classes and inheritance are not part of Slug."
not-supported methods "method declarations are not part of Slug; use modules + functions + structs."
not-supported macros "a macro system is intentionally absent unless explicitly added later."
not-supported try-catch "try/catch is not part of the stable language contract."
not-supported implicit-globals "there are no implicit global bindings."

@section agent-idioms
idiom val-over-var "strongly prefer val over var; use var only when a binding genuinely requires reassignment within its scope; val is the default."
idiom recursion "prefer local step/recur helpers for traversal."
idiom accumulators "prefer explicit accumulator parameters over hidden state."
idiom reducers "use stdlib reducers when they make code simpler and the API is confirmed."
idiom maps "prefer symbol-keyed maps for internal records and options."
idiom updates "prefer put/remove/update/copy{} for persistent updates."
idiom imports "prefer named imports val { f, g } = import(...) when only a few names are needed; use wildcard val {*} = import(...) when many names are needed."
idiom contracts "prefer exported/public APIs from MANIFEST.ai over inferring internals from source."
idiom errors "prefer consistent error style within a module."
idiom clarity "write straightforward recursive code before clever code."
idiom docs "authored library exports should include doc metadata/comments where the ecosystem supports it."
idiom chain-over-nesting "prefer call-chain style (x /> f /> g) over nested calls (g(f(x))) for sequential data transforms."
idiom chain-parens "omit parentheses for single-parameter calls in a chain: x /> trim /> toLower not x /> trim() /> toLower()."
idiom inline-fn "fn(x) { expr } is valid as a call-chain step for short single-expression transforms; extract a named val for anything complex."
idiom interpolation "use {{expr}} string interpolation for embedding values in strings rather than concatenation when it improves readability."
idiom top-level-match "top-level match expressions are valid statements; val _ = match ... is not required."
idiom block-exprs "intermediate expressions in blocks do not need to be bound to _; they are valid statements."
idiom recur-arity "recur must pass all parameters of the enclosing function including those with defaults."
idiom json-dot-access "prefer dot access (m.name) over bracket access when working with JSON-decoded maps; slug.json#decode produces string-keyed maps so m[:name] always returns nil on decoded data."
idiom bracket-when-explicit "use bracket syntax m[\"key\"] or m[:key] only when key type precision matters or keys are dynamic."
idiom forward-reference "Slug is sequential; define functions before calling them; never forward-reference a val or var binding."
idiom main-pattern "prefer @main val main = fn(args = argm()) { ... } as the entrypoint pattern; passing argm() as a default parameter keeps the function testable without special runner support."
idiom top-level-side-effects "keep import-time top-level side effects minimal; reserve I/O and startup logic for @main so that importing the module as a library does not trigger program behaviour."

@section examples
example recursion "
val sum = fn(xs) {
  val step = fn(xs, acc) {
    match xs {
      [] => acc
      [h, ...t] => recur(t, acc + h)
    }
  }
  step(xs, 0)
}
"

example map-update "
val flags = { l: false, w: false }
val flags2 = flags /> put(:l, true)
"

example struct-copy "
val User = struct { name, age = 0 }
val a = User{name: \"eva\"}
val b = a copy{age: 42}
"

example match "
val classify = fn(n) {
  match n {
    0 => :zero
    1 => :one
    _ => :many
  }
}
"

example match-in-call-chain "
// match as a pipeline decision boundary — consumes the prior value as scrutinee
input
/> parse
/> validate
/> match {
  {ok: v}    => save(v)
  {error: e} => report(e)
}

// match requires an explicit subject outside of pipeline position
val describe = fn(x) {
  match x {
    _ if x > 100 => :large
    _ if x > 10  => :medium
    _            => :small
  }
}
"

example defer "
val f = fn(v) {
  defer { println(\"run on exit\") }
  defer onsuccess { println(\"run on success\") }
  defer onerror(err) {
    println(\"run on failure\", stacktrace(err))
    v + 1 // f call result on error; can also rethrow with throw err
  }
  throw v
}
f(1) // => 2
"

example interpolation "
val name = \"Slug\"
val greeting = \"Hello, {{name}}!\"    // => \"Hello, Slug!\"
val multi = \"\"\"
  Welcome to {{name}}
  version {{version}}
\"\"\"
// raw strings do not interpolate — use for SQL, regex, content with braces
val query = 'select * from {{table}}'  // literal string, not interpolated
"

example call-chain "
// preferred: chain style
path
/> replaceAll('/', '.')
/> trim('.')
/> split('.')

// avoid: nested style
split(trim(replaceAll(path, '/', '.'), '.'), '.')

// inline fn step for short transform
content /> trim /> mkQueryFunction

// parentheses optional on single-param calls — both equivalent
x /> trim /> toLower
x /> trim() /> toLower()
"

example struct-destructure "
val SqlQuery = struct { @str sql, @list args, @sym type }
val result = extractArgs(rawSql)
val SqlQuery{ sql, args: queryParams, type: kind } = result
// sql, queryParams, and kind are now bound
"

example import-forms "
// named import — preferred when few names needed
val { readFile, writeFile } = import('slug.io.fs')

// wildcard import — use when many names needed
val {*} = import('slug.std', 'slug.string', 'slug.list')

// module import — for qualified access
val json = import('slug.json')
json.encode(value)
"

example cmdargs "
// called with: slug cli.slug -ab --name knuckles input.txt
val args = argv() // [\"input.txt\"]
val opts = argm() // { options: { a:true, b:true, name:\"knuckles\" }, positional: [\"input.txt\"] }
"

example spread-call "
val add = fn(a, b, c) { a + b + c }
val nums = [1, 2, 3]

add(...nums)   // 6  — spreads list into three separate arguments
add(nums)      // ERROR: add expects 3 args, got 1 (a LIST)

// common pattern: a @list of functions passed to a variadic parameter
val applyAll = fn(@list ls, ...fns) {
  ls /> map(fn(h) { fns /> map(fn(f) { f(h) }) })
}
val fns = [fn(a){a}, fn(b){b+1}]
applyAll([1,2], ...fns)   // spread fns into variadic ...fns param
applyAll([1,2], fns)      // WRONG: fns arrives as a single LIST arg, not two fn args
"

example main "
// idiomatic @main — args as default parameter for testability
@main
val main = fn(args = argm()) {
  val result = args /> argsWith(spec)
  match result {
    { action: :help }                 => { println(result.text); import('slug.sys').exit(0) }
    { action: :version }              => { println(result.text); import('slug.sys').exit(0) }
    { action: :error }                => { result.errors /> map(fn(e) { println(e.msg) }); import('slug.sys').exit(1) }
    { ok: true, options, positional } => run(options, positional)
  }
}

// @main never runs on import — safe to import this module as a library
// top-level statements above main still execute on import (wiring, registration etc.)
"

@section agent-rules
agent rule "Do not invent syntax not stated in SLUG.ai."
agent rule "Do not invent builtins not listed in SLUG.ai or a loaded MANIFEST.ai."
agent rule "Do not assume stdlib function names beyond what MANIFEST.ai confirms for the target library."
agent rule "Prefer public MANIFEST.ai contracts over reading implementation details."
agent rule "Respect immutability; use persistent update helpers rather than mutation."
agent rule "No loops; express iteration using recur, match, and reducers."
agent rule "Use structured concurrency forms only; avoid unscoped background tasks."
agent rule "When CLI behavior matters, do not guess argv/argm shape beyond what this file or MANIFEST.ai states."
agent rule "When generating code, prefer simple idiomatic Slug over importing patterns from other languages."
agent rule "Prefer val over var; only use var when a binding genuinely requires reassignment within its scope."
agent rule "String interpolation {{expr}} is available inside string literals and multi-line strings; prefer it over concatenation for readability."
agent rule "val {*} = import(...) is valid; prefer named imports val { f } = import(...) when only a few names are needed."
agent rule "JSON-decoded maps have string keys; always use dot access or m[\"key\"] on decoded data; m[:key] always returns nil on JSON-decoded maps."
agent rule "Map literals in match arm return position need double braces: => {{ k: v }} not => { k: v }."
agent rule "recur must pass all parameters including those with defaults; omitting any parameter is a runtime error."
agent rule "Multi-statement match arm bodies always require braces: pattern => { stmt; stmt } not pattern => stmt; stmt."
agent rule "Slug is sequential — functions must be defined before they are called; never forward-reference a val binding."
agent rule "Prefer call-chain style (x /> f /> g) over nested calls (g(f(x))) for sequential data transforms."
agent rule "Parentheses are optional for single-parameter calls in a chain: x /> trim not x /> trim()."
agent rule "match requires an explicit scrutinee except in pipeline position: value /> match { ... } is valid; match { ... } as a standalone expression is not valid."
agent rule "match may appear as a pipeline stage: value /> match { ... } consumes the prior value as scrutinee; use as a terminal decision boundary where a single value fans into distinct outcomes."
agent rule "@main never executes on import; it only executes when the module is the running module."
agent rule "@main must be callable with zero arguments; use default parameters to receive runtime inputs: @main val main = fn(args = argm()) { ... }"
agent rule "A module may have at most one @main; defining multiple @main functions is a compilation error."
agent rule "Keep top-level statements side-effect-free where possible; put I/O and startup logic in @main so the module is safe to import as a library."
agent rule "To pass a @list value to a variadic ...rest parameter, spread it at the call site: f(...myList) not f(myList). Passing a list directly as a single argument is not the same as spreading it — f(myList) passes one argument of type LIST, f(...myList) passes N arguments."
